# D50 (2026-08-28): `unsafe` is REMOVED from the language, so the word is now
# an ORDINARY IDENTIFIER. The removal is a pure WIDENING on every position a
# reserved word blocked — this fixture is one cell per position, so a partial
# re-reservation (a stray keyword row, a parser special-case) trips it.
#
# AXIS: source positions where an identifier is legal.
#   local variable name .... `int unsafe = 5`      (was: "`unsafe` is a reserved keyword")
#   struct field name ...... `int unsafe`          (was: "expected identifier")
#   parameter name ......... `int unsafe`          (was: parse error)
#   function name .......... `int unsafe(...)`     (was: parse error)
#   scope label ............ `unsafe:`             (was: `Stmt::Unsafe`)
#   field access ........... `s.unsafe`
# OMITTED CELL: `unsafe` as a TYPE name — Gorget type names are capitalised by
# convention and the lexer draws no keyword distinction there, so it samples
# the same lexer cell as the local-variable row.
#
# RED-verified against the pre-removal compiler built from f3feea79:
# `gg check` -> rc 1, **6 parse errors across 5 of the 6 cells** —
# "expected identifier, found 'unsafe'" (field, param), "expected type,
# found INDENT" (the cascade from the broken fn header), "`unsafe` is a
# reserved keyword and cannot be used as a variable name" (local), and
# "expected expression, found 'unsafe'" (both reads). Only the scope-label
# cell parsed pre-removal, and it parsed as a DIFFERENT construct.
struct Holder:
    int unsafe

int unsafe(int unsafe):
    return unsafe + 1

# The call lives in its own function: a local named `unsafe` SHADOWS the
# function of the same name, which is ordinary scoping and not a D50 cell.
void call_the_function():
    print(unsafe(1))

void main():
    int unsafe = 5
    print(unsafe)
    Holder h = Holder(7)
    print(h.unsafe)
    call_the_function()
    unsafe:
        print(9)
